From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:25:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Ask for login input through a chat when the UI has no request
 dialogs

Signing in is interactive - a code, possibly a 2FA password, and for a new
account a display name - and each is asked for with purple_request_input().
A front-end that installs no request ui-ops gets NULL back, and the plugin
then either gives up ("Authentication code is required but this libpurple
doesn't support input requests") or, for the login code, does not notice at
all and waits forever.

Fall back to asking in an IM conversation instead: the prompt is delivered
as an ordinary incoming message from a buddy called "Telegram", through
serv_got_im() so that it takes the same receive path as every other message
(privacy check, receiving-im-msg/received-im-msg, conversation creation,
PURPLE_MESSAGE_RECV). A message the user sends to "Telegram" while an
answer is pending is taken as that answer, stripped of markup, and handed to
the callback the dialog would have called; it is not sent to anyone.
Messages to anyone else go out as usual, and OTR negotiation messages are
ignored while waiting.

While a value is awaited, that conversation is not logged, and the password
is not echoed back into it (send_im returns 0). A front-end that shows what
is typed by itself still shows it, so the password prompt says it will be
visible on screen.

This is what lets an account be signed in from the webOS Messaging app, whose
libpurple host (imlibpurpletransport) deliberately installs no request
ui-ops.

This is the pull request as it stands after review, squashed; it takes in:
- 9fdd6ac "Take login input only from the "Telegram" chat, and keep it out of the log" (Herman van Hazendonk)

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://github.com/UnitedMarsupials/tdlib-purple/pull/31]
